<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hengist and Horsa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Hengest and Horsa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hengist and Horsa are legendary brothers said to have led the first Germanic settlers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutes">Jutes</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the 5th century. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>, they came around 449 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> of the British ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vortigern">Vortigern</a> and then rebelled against him; Horsa was killed in battle, and Hengist founded the kingdom of Kent. Most historians treat the story as a foundation legend rather than history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hengist-and-horsa.jpg" alt="Hengist and Horsa" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sir Edward Parrott · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hengist_and_Horsa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The Old English names Hengest and Horsa mean stallion and horse. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, writing in the 8th century, makes the brothers sons of Wictgils, son of Witta, son of Wecta, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woden">Woden</a>, and the 9th-century History of the Britons attributed to Nennius gives a longer line reaching back to Geta, whom it calls the offspring of a heathen idol. Modern scholars regard the pair as mythical, pointing to their alliterative animal names, their seemingly constructed genealogy and the unknown nature of Bede's sources.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vortigern">Vortigern</a> invites the brothers to fight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>; they land at Ebbsfleet, defeat the Picts and send home for more men. Under 455 it records that they fought Vortigern at Aylesford, where Horsa died, and that Hengist then ruled with his son Esc. Further victories follow at Crayford in 457, where four thousand Britons are said to have been killed, and in 465, and the last entry, for 473, has the Britons fleeing from the English like fire.</p><p>The History of the Britons tells a fuller story. Hengist wins Kent by giving his daughter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vortigern">Vortigern</a> in marriage, sends for his son Ochta and Ebissa, and is driven back to Thanet by Vortigern's son Vortimer. At a feast meant to seal a peace he has his men hide knives, gives the signal nima der sexa and massacres the British leaders, the episode known as the Treachery of the Long Knives. In the 12th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-of-monmouth">Geoffrey of Monmouth</a> expanded the tale, naming the daughter Rowena.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In Geoffrey's version Hengist is captured by Eldol, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a>, after a battle near Kaerconan and beheaded. Tradition placed his burial at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hengistbury-head">Hengistbury Head</a>, but its mounds date to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. Rudolf Simek sees horse-head gables in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, still called Hengst und Hors around 1875, as a sign that the pair were first horse-shaped mythological beings, while J. R. R. Tolkien argued that the Hengest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beowulf">Beowulf</a> was a historical man. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> proposed the brothers for the Great Seal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1776, and two British gliders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> bore their names.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hengist and Horsa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bede says Kent was settled by Jutes led by <b>Hengest and Horsa</b>, who came as mercenaries of Vortigern around 449 and then turned against the Britons.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kent">Kingdom of Kent</a></li></ul></section>
